{
  "id": "Compound::DB06694 - Gene::146",
  "verbalization": "Xylometazoline, a nasal decongestant, is shown to bind to Adra1a and Adra1b, which are subtypes of the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor. These receptors are involved in the regulation of alpha-1 adrenergic receptor activity, a pathway that is also engaged by Adra1d. The evidence suggests that Xylometazoline may exert its effects through a common mechanism involving the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor, with Adra1d being a downstream participant in this pathway. The scores from the paths indicate a strong, consistent relationship between Xylometazoline and Adra1d, suggesting that the drug may modulate the activity of the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor through a shared biological mechanism. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Xylometazoline to influence the function of Adra1d, potentially through the activation or inhibition of the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor. The observed evidence supports a mechanistic link between Xylometazoline and Adra1d, emphasizing the role of the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor in mediating the drug's effects.",
